---
import ContentLayout from '../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
---
<ContentLayout title="Editorial Methodology | StackGeist" description="How StackGeist records product ownership, verifies claims, evaluates fit, and keeps affiliate relationships separate from recommendations.">
  <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">StackGeist</a> / Editorial methodology</p>
  <p class="page-kicker">Editorial standards</p>
  <h1 class="page-title">Show the evidence. Name the tradeoff. Let the reader decide.</h1>
  <p class="page-lead">StackGeist evaluates products around the problem they solve, the setup they fit, and the reasons a reader should skip them. Evidence labels make the basis for each page explicit.</p>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="page-kicker">Evidence states</p>
    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card"><h2>Owned &amp; used</h2><p>The StackGeist operator personally purchased and used the product. This establishes ownership and practical familiarity. It is not automatically a laboratory test, a durability guarantee, or proof of every technical specification.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h2>Researched</h2><p>The recommendation is based on identified manufacturer documentation, standards, manuals, or retailer identity records. StackGeist does not claim personal ownership or hands-on testing in this state.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="page-kicker">Selection</p>
    <h2 class="section-heading">Fit comes before the retailer button.</h2>
    <p>A recommendation begins with a specific constraint: dimensions, ports, power, compatibility, workflow, budget, or another concrete setup problem. Pages should explain who the product fits, what to check before buying, the meaningful tradeoffs, and who should skip it.</p>
    <p>Products are not ranked because they carry an affiliate link. A purchase option is added only after the page can explain a defensible use case.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="page-kicker">Source hierarchy</p>
    <h2 class="section-heading">Technical claims remain traceable.</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Manufacturer documentation and product manuals for specifications and compatibility.</li>
      <li>Standards bodies and platform documentation for protocol behavior.</li>
      <li>Retailer pages for exact model, ASIN, current availability, and the purchase destination.</li>
      <li>Independent measurements when the method and tested model are clear.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Prices and inventory change. StackGeist does not treat a previously observed price as guaranteed.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="page-kicker">Affiliate independence</p>
    <h2 class="section-heading">Compensation does not erase the reasons to skip.</h2>
    <p>Some retailer links can earn StackGeist a commission. Qualifying links are disclosed nearby. Affiliate eligibility does not determine the evidence state, recommendation, comparison winner, or stated tradeoffs.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="page-kicker">Updates and Corrections</p>
    <h2 class="section-heading">Review dates are records, not freshness theater.</h2>
    <p>An evidence record shows when its supporting sources were last reviewed. Material corrections are made when a model identity, specification, compatibility statement, ownership label, or purchase link is shown to be wrong.</p>
    <p>To report a correction, use the route listed on the <a href="/contact">Contact page</a> and include the page URL, disputed statement, and supporting source.</p>
  </section>
</ContentLayout>
